2026-10-07T02:06:15Z result (pod qxtlst14nfrztt, 5x A6000 $2.65/h, 01:50:22-02:05:08, ~$0.65): kills at 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7 of a step detected by the slot in 43-46 ms; at 0.9 by the sentinel at 0.213 s. The stack files stayed empty (vLLM's workers wrote the faulthandler dumps to the run log, three interleaved); read from the log: at 0.9 every survivor's step thread was idle in vLLM's worker loop (worker_busy_loop > dequeue > acquire_read), waiting for the executor's next RPC. The survivors had finished the step; the executor waited for the dead rank's answer, which never came. Not the logits all-gather: a kill late in a step leaves no survivor in any collective. At 0.5 one survivor was in synchronize, waiting on the slot kernel. The stall rule (executor in flight, no step ending) covers this case.
